WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.863
Part one turned a slanted basis

2
00:00:05.959 --> 00:00:08.062
into orthonormal axes.

3
00:00:08.062 --> 00:00:11.121
Now run Gram-Schmidt backwards

4
00:00:11.216 --> 00:00:13.606
each original vector is a

5
00:00:13.701 --> 00:00:15.900
combination of the q's.

6
00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:21.169
Take a one, one point five, two, and a two

7
00:00:21.169 --> 00:00:23.000
minus one, two.

8
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.232
Gram-Schmidt gives q one, point six

9
00:00:27.232 --> 00:00:32.152
point eight, and q two, minus point eight

10
00:00:32.152 --> 00:00:33.410
point six.

11
00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:37.505
a one is two and a half q one.

12
00:00:37.505 --> 00:00:42.000
a two is one q one, plus two q two.

13
00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:45.679
Write those amounts into a matrix R.

14
00:00:45.679 --> 00:00:49.470
One corner, below the diagonal, is zero.

15
00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:52.871
Why must that corner be zero?

16
00:00:52.871 --> 00:00:54.660
Pause and think.

17
00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:01.742
Because a one is built first

18
00:01:01.742 --> 00:01:03.915
from q one alone.

19
00:01:03.915 --> 00:01:08.866
Column j of A only uses q one up to q j

20
00:01:08.866 --> 00:01:11.160
never a later one.

21
00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:15.758
So any matrix with independent columns

22
00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:18.553
factors as A equals Q R.

23
00:01:18.553 --> 00:01:21.572
Q has orthonormal columns

24
00:01:21.572 --> 00:01:24.590
and R is upper triangular.

25
00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:28.378
Watch the two moves on the unit square.

26
00:01:28.378 --> 00:01:31.817
First R: stretch along the first axis

27
00:01:31.817 --> 00:01:33.668
and lean the second.

28
00:01:33.668 --> 00:01:35.520
Then Q: a pure turn

29
00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:37.900
with no stretching at all.

30
00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:42.111
The square lands exactly where A puts it

31
00:01:42.111 --> 00:01:45.690
with its sides along a one and a two.

32
00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:49.697
Elimination gave A equals L U.

33
00:01:49.697 --> 00:01:53.720
Gram-Schmidt gives A equals Q R.

34
00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.874
Now solve A x equals b.

35
00:01:56.874 --> 00:02:00.119
Take b to be one half, four

36
00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:04.260
the far corner of the warped square.

37
00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:06.712
Multiply by Q transpose.

38
00:02:06.712 --> 00:02:09.776
That turns the whole picture back

39
00:02:09.776 --> 00:02:12.316
and b becomes Q transpose b

40
00:02:12.316 --> 00:02:14.330
three and a half, two.

41
00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:17.652
A turn changes no coordinates.

42
00:02:17.652 --> 00:02:20.525
So R x equals Q transpose b

43
00:02:20.525 --> 00:02:22.605
a triangular system

44
00:02:22.605 --> 00:02:25.280
solved from the bottom up.

45
00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:29.172
The last row says two x two is two

46
00:02:29.172 --> 00:02:30.892
so x two is one.

47
00:02:30.892 --> 00:02:34.636
The first says two and a half x one

48
00:02:34.636 --> 00:02:39.190
plus one, is three and a half: x one is one.

49
00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:42.370
Least squares goes the same way.

50
00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:45.466
Put Q R into the normal equations.

51
00:02:45.466 --> 00:02:48.208
Q transpose Q is the identity

52
00:02:48.208 --> 00:02:50.065
R transpose cancels

53
00:02:50.065 --> 00:02:53.780
and R x hat equals Q transpose b is left.

54
00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:57.347
For the café of Lecture fourteen

55
00:02:57.347 --> 00:02:58.976
A is four by two.

56
00:02:58.976 --> 00:03:01.872
q one is a half times all ones

57
00:03:01.962 --> 00:03:04.858
and the weeks minus their shadow

58
00:03:04.948 --> 00:03:08.840
are the centred weeks, of length root five.

59
00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:13.385
So R is two, three, zero, root five

60
00:03:13.502 --> 00:03:16.532
and Q transpose b is eight

61
00:03:16.648 --> 00:03:19.561
and seven over root five.

62
00:03:19.561 --> 00:03:23.990
Back substitution: D is seven fifths

63
00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:27.019
then C is one point nine.

64
00:03:27.019 --> 00:03:29.350
The same best line.

65
00:03:29.930 --> 00:03:33.435
The same R hides inside A transpose A.

66
00:03:33.435 --> 00:03:36.018
Turn back with Q transpose

67
00:03:36.018 --> 00:03:37.770
what is left is R.

68
00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:40.158
Now apply R transpose

69
00:03:40.158 --> 00:03:42.659
which is lower triangular.

70
00:03:42.752 --> 00:03:44.975
The square lands where A

71
00:03:45.067 --> 00:03:46.920
transpose A puts it.

72
00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:51.368
So A transpose A equals R transpose R

73
00:03:51.368 --> 00:03:53.957
the turn has cancelled.

74
00:03:53.957 --> 00:03:56.761
Call L equal R transpose

75
00:03:56.761 --> 00:04:01.290
and A transpose A is L times L transpose

76
00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:04.310
the Cholesky factorization.

77
00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:08.860
Cholesky needs a positive definite matrix

78
00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:11.260
which A transpose A is.

79
00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:14.760
One, two, two, one is not: at one

80
00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:17.860
minus one, it gives minus two.

81
00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:21.852
So why not always form A transpose A

82
00:04:21.852 --> 00:04:23.511
and use Cholesky?

83
00:04:23.511 --> 00:04:27.476
It is cheaper, and in exact arithmetic the

84
00:04:27.568 --> 00:04:29.320
answer is the same.

85
00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:32.930
On a computer it is not the same.

86
00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:36.787
Here are two columns that nearly line up.

87
00:04:36.787 --> 00:04:40.827
They squeeze the square into a thin sliver

88
00:04:40.827 --> 00:04:44.500
about ten times longer than it is wide.

89
00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:47.231
Forming A transpose A applies

90
00:04:47.313 --> 00:04:48.791
the squeeze twice

91
00:04:48.791 --> 00:04:51.008
now it is about a hundred.

92
00:04:51.008 --> 00:04:53.964
That ratio is the condition number

93
00:04:53.964 --> 00:04:57.330
how much small rounding errors can grow.

94
00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:00.057
For a degree seven polynomial

95
00:05:00.139 --> 00:05:02.024
fit to eleven readings

96
00:05:02.024 --> 00:05:05.057
it is ten to the eight for the data

97
00:05:05.057 --> 00:05:08.499
and ten to the sixteen for A transpose A.

98
00:05:08.581 --> 00:05:11.122
That uses up all sixteen digits

99
00:05:11.204 --> 00:05:12.597
a computer keeps.

100
00:05:12.597 --> 00:05:14.400
Q R never squares it.

101
00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:18.576
So stable regression has two habits.

102
00:05:18.576 --> 00:05:21.572
Solve least squares with Q R

103
00:05:21.572 --> 00:05:25.668
or with the S V D of Lecture twenty-six

104
00:05:25.668 --> 00:05:29.363
as NumPy and statistics packages do.

105
00:05:29.363 --> 00:05:32.060
Do not form X transpose X.

106
00:05:32.410 --> 00:05:34.777
And standardise the features.

107
00:05:34.859 --> 00:05:37.716
Centring makes the intercept column

108
00:05:37.797 --> 00:05:40.083
perpendicular to the others

109
00:05:40.083 --> 00:05:43.675
the first Gram-Schmidt step, done for free.

110
00:05:43.675 --> 00:05:46.450
The squeezed grid opens up again.

111
00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:50.733
So: A equals Q R records Gram-Schmidt

112
00:05:50.733 --> 00:05:54.143
a turn after a triangular stretch.

113
00:05:54.143 --> 00:05:58.430
Least squares becomes one triangular solve.

114
00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:01.511
A transpose A equals R transpose

115
00:06:01.596 --> 00:06:03.303
R is Cholesky: fast

116
00:06:03.303 --> 00:06:06.460
but it squares the condition number.

117
00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:09.753
Next lecture starts a new number

118
00:06:09.837 --> 00:06:12.974
for a square matrix: the determinant.

119
00:06:12.974 --> 00:06:15.517
This Q only turns the square

120
00:06:15.517 --> 00:06:17.467
so its area stays one.

121
00:06:17.467 --> 00:06:19.840
What does R do to the area?
